1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,634
A tangent line gives a simple estimate near a known input.

2
00:00:03,904 --> 00:00:07,027
Choose sixteen because its square root is exactly four.

3
00:00:07,297 --> 00:00:11,384
The approximation uses the function value and slope at that easy point.

4
00:00:12,504 --> 00:00:16,045
The square root derivative is one over two square root x.

5
00:00:16,315 --> 00:00:18,753
At sixteen, the slope is one eighth.

6
00:00:19,023 --> 00:00:23,597
This slope converts a small input change into an estimated output change.

7
00:00:24,717 --> 00:00:26,250
Build the tangent line.

8
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:30,514
The change in x must be measured from sixteen, not from zero.

9
00:00:30,784 --> 00:00:35,996
This is an approximation to the square root function, rather than a new exact formula for it.

10
00:00:37,116 --> 00:00:40,123
The input increases by zero point zero one.

11
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:43,145
Divide this change by eight and add four.

12
00:00:43,415 --> 00:00:46,643
Our estimate is four point zero zero one two five.

13
00:00:47,763 --> 00:00:52,743
The square root curve is concave down, so its tangent line lies above the curve.

14
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:55,126
Our estimate is slightly too high.

15
00:00:55,396 --> 00:01:00,214
The actual value is about four point zero zero one two four nine eight.

16
00:01:00,484 --> 00:01:04,374
The approximation works well because the input is close to sixteen.

17
00:01:05,494 --> 00:01:09,162
Choose the easy nearby input two, not one point nine.

18
00:01:09,432 --> 00:01:14,506
The change from two to one point nine nine nine is negative zero point zero zero one.

19
00:01:15,626 --> 00:01:21,710
The tangent approximation uses the exact function value sixteen and the slope thirty two at the base point.

20
00:01:22,830 --> 00:01:26,626
Multiply slope by input change and add the base value.

21
00:01:26,896 --> 00:01:33,409
Because x to the fourth is concave up near two, the tangent estimate is slightly below the exact value.

22
00:01:34,529 --> 00:01:38,407
A differential is d y equals the derivative times d x.

23
00:01:38,677 --> 00:01:44,076
It gives the linearized output change, rather than the exact change in the function.

24
00:01:45,196 --> 00:01:50,223
Differentiate the polynomial, keeping the constant term derivative equal to zero.

25
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:56,033
Evaluate the derivative at the given x before multiplying by the given d x.

26
00:01:56,303 --> 00:01:58,974
The requested differential is zero point eight.

27
00:02:00,094 --> 00:02:03,890
Use differentials to estimate how an edge measurement error propagates.

28
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:08,421
We want the magnitude of the possible error, so use absolute values.

29
00:02:09,541 --> 00:02:13,767
The derivative of cube volume with respect to its edge is three a squared.

30
00:02:14,037 --> 00:02:20,315
Multiplying by the maximum edge error gives an estimated maximum volume error of two hundred seventy cubic

31
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:21,026
centimeters.

32
00:02:22,146 --> 00:02:26,546
Relative error divides the estimated absolute error by the computed volume.

33
00:02:26,816 --> 00:02:29,963
It is zero point zero one, which is one percent.

34
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:34,482
Differential estimates are first-order estimates, not exact error bounds.

35
00:02:35,602 --> 00:02:40,141
For part b, use the surface-area formula instead of the volume formula.

36
00:02:40,411 --> 00:02:42,130
The derivative is twelve a.

37
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:47,975
The estimated maximum surface-area error is thirty six square centimeters.

38
00:02:48,245 --> 00:02:50,497
The units are area units this time.

39
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:58,548
The relative area error is about zero point zero zero six six seven, or zero point six six seven percent.

40
00:02:58,818 --> 00:03:04,956
The relative volume error is three times the relative edge error; the relative area error is twice the relative

41
00:03:04,956 --> 00:03:05,564
edge error.

42
00:03:06,684 --> 00:03:08,019
Part a is false.

43
00:03:08,289 --> 00:03:11,296
E squared is a constant, not e to the x.

44
00:03:11,566 --> 00:03:14,515
Its derivative with respect to x is zero.

45
00:03:14,785 --> 00:03:19,046
The proposed answer two e would come from treating the constant e as a variable.

46
00:03:20,166 --> 00:03:21,629
Part b is false.

47
00:03:21,899 --> 00:03:27,553
X is in the exponent, so use the exponential rule, not the constant exponent power rule.

48
00:03:27,823 --> 00:03:31,724
The correct derivative is ten to the x times natural log ten.

49
00:03:32,844 --> 00:03:34,306
Part c is false.

50
00:03:34,576 --> 00:03:38,768
Natural log ten is another constant, so its derivative is zero.

51
00:03:39,038 --> 00:03:43,473
The formula one over x applies to natural log x when x varies.

52
00:03:44,593 --> 00:03:45,847
Part d is true.

53
00:03:46,117 --> 00:03:50,540
The polynomial has degree thirty, since the highest power is x to the thirtieth.

54
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:53,341
Each derivative lowers its degree by one.

55
00:03:53,611 --> 00:03:56,815
After thirty one derivatives, every term is zero.

56
00:03:57,935 --> 00:04:01,859
Part e is the correct chain rule wherever f of x is positive.

57
00:04:02,129 --> 00:04:05,009
Under that usual domain assumption it is true.

58
00:04:05,279 --> 00:04:12,394
As literally stated with only differentiability of f, it needs a qualification: differentiability alone does not

59
00:04:12,394 --> 00:04:14,300
guarantee this formula exists.

60
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:22,983
For example, f of x equals x squared is differentiable everywhere, but the square root of f is absolute x, which

61
00:04:22,983 --> 00:04:25,009
is not differentiable at zero.

62
00:04:25,279 --> 00:04:28,832
This explains why the positive-inside condition matters.

63
00:04:29,952 --> 00:04:31,450
Part f is false.

64
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:37,513
The outside derivative must be evaluated at the inside input square root x, not at x.

65
00:04:38,633 --> 00:04:42,894
The correct formula has f prime of square root x in the numerator.

66
00:04:43,164 --> 00:04:48,656
If f of u is u squared, the original composition is x and its derivative is one.

67
00:04:48,926 --> 00:04:53,337
The incorrect formula gives square root x, which is not always one.

68
00:04:54,457 --> 00:04:55,746
Part g is true.

69
00:04:56,016 --> 00:04:59,929
This is the sum rule: differentiate each summand separately.

70
00:05:01,049 --> 00:05:02,453
Part h is false.

71
00:05:02,723 --> 00:05:07,414
The product rule is f prime times g, plus f times g prime.

72
00:05:07,684 --> 00:05:11,062
For a counterexample, let both functions equal x.

73
00:05:11,332 --> 00:05:17,161
The product is x squared and its derivative is two x, but multiplying the derivatives gives one.

74
00:05:18,281 --> 00:05:19,511
Part i is true.

75
00:05:19,781 --> 00:05:26,585
This is the chain rule, with the outside derivative evaluated at the unchanged inside function, then multiplied

76
00:05:26,585 --> 00:05:28,117
by the inside derivative.

77
00:05:29,237 --> 00:05:30,503
Part j is false.

78
00:05:30,773 --> 00:05:33,524
It uses the wrong input for f prime.

79
00:05:33,794 --> 00:05:37,765
Take f of u equal to u squared and g of x equal to x squared.

80
00:05:38,035 --> 00:05:44,680
The composition is x to the fourth, so its derivative is four x cubed, while the proposed expression is four x

81
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:45,163
squared.

